#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Ship a worktree's commits: rebase, check, then push, or with --local land them on
the main tree's branch.

    wt-ship                       rebase onto origin/<main tree's branch>, check, push
    wt-ship --remove              then remove this worktree, through the main tree
    wt-ship --remote up --branch dev
    wt-ship --local [--remove]    in a repo that doesn't push: land on the main tree
    wt-ship -C ../repo-wt-topic --local --remove
                                  the same, run from outside the worktree

In order, it:

1. refuses uncommitted changes to tracked files, and a worktree whose earlier commits
   another run is checking, or where one was killed before it put the tip back (step 4);
2. fetches the remote, and when HEAD lacks REMOTE/BRANCH or holds fixup! commits,
   rebases onto it, folding them (autosquash). A conflict stops it with the rebase in
   progress: resolve the hunks (`conflicts -h`), finish with `conflicts --continue`,
   then run it again;
3. refuses leftover conflict markers in what it would push, and a commit message that
   names, by its ID, a commit the branch doesn't hold, as when a message cites an
   earlier commit of the same landing, whose ID a rebase changes: a word of 7 to 64
   lowercase hex digits that `git rev-parse --verify WORD^{commit}` resolves to a
   commit the branch doesn't hold, whether from before a rebase or after it. A hex
   word that resolves to no commit here, such as another repo's commit, is ignored.
   So is the ID on a whole line `(cherry picked from commit ID)`, which `git
   cherry-pick -x` adds and whose source is meant to stay off the branch, when a ref
   that lasts holds that commit. A branch, a tag or a remote-tracking ref lasts; a
   branch a linked worktree has checked out doesn't, nor does its twin on a remote
   (refs/remotes/REMOTE/BRANCH): they are a session's work, which its landing rebases.
   Nor does any ref count for a commit of this landing's own, which HEAD holds or
   whose patch another of its commits has, as the copy from before a rebase does. The
   same ID on any other line still counts, as do wider citations of a held commit
   ("Backport of ID", the `commit ID` lines of `git merge --squash`). When no ref at
   all holds a cherry-pick line's source, the refusal says how to give it one (a tag,
   or a fetch) if it should last.
   It lists each such commit with the IDs it names, newest first, and the fix for
   each, `build-state reword SHA`, to name the commit by its subject instead, run in
   the order listed. This runs after every rebase, so before any check, and with
   --local before the wait;
4. checks every commit it would push with the repo's agent-pre-commit hook, else none,
   and says so: each commit whose tree has no pass of the check, as its bytes are now,
   on agent-commit-gate's record, HEAD included, oldest first, each checked out in
   turn here, forced. Each runs through agent-commit-gate --run, which hands the check
   CHECK_BASE, usually the commit before or the main branch's tip (`agent-commit-gate
   -h`), so it can run only what that commit changed. Two rounds: first each commit
   with --no-queue, which runs a check that needs no build or test, such as a
   docs-only commit's, outside heavy's queue; then the commits whose check needs the
   queue, all in one heavy job, so no other session's job runs between them, at the
   queue's front with --local, which checks only under the main tree's claim. The
   first failure stops it, naming the commit to fix with a fixup!, and
   says when the branch's tip it rebased onto has no pass either. While the work tree
   holds an earlier commit, a marker in the worktree's git dir names the tip and this
   run, and agent-commit-gate refuses commits there, which would be lost; the tip comes
   back and the marker goes however the checks end, unless the run is killed outright,
   and then the next run names the tip to put back. A repo that keeps the check in its
   tree (core.hooksPath inside it) fails a commit that drops it while the branch has
   it: the commit must hold an executable file there, or a link that leads to one in
   the commit's own tree;
5. pushes HEAD to REMOTE/BRANCH. When another session's push wins the race (a
   non-fast-forward rejection), it fetches, rebases, checks and pushes again, up to
   10 tries in all, then stops saying to run it again;
6. fetches again and confirms the remote branch holds HEAD;
7. prints the range it pushed, a notice for other sessions (which it doesn't send),
   and whether the main tree's branch is now behind the remote.

BRANCH defaults to the branch the main worktree has checked out. Nothing to push is a
clean exit. --remove runs `git worktree remove` from the main tree once the push is
confirmed. A shell that started the run inside the worktree is left in a deleted
folder, where Claude Code's Bash tool reports exit 1 after a landing that worked; -C
DIR, which runs wt-ship as if started in DIR (as `git -C` does), lets the call start
outside it.

--local, for a repo whose owner pushes, takes the same steps against the branch the
main tree has checked out instead of a remote (no --remote, and no other --branch):
it rebases onto that branch as above, replaying only the commits since this
worktree forked from it (`--fork-point`, from the branch's reflog): a commit the branch
held then and holds no more, one another session rewrote or dropped, isn't replayed,
and the run names each such commit. With no fork point in the reflog it rebases as a
plain rebase would. It checks nothing yet: another landing may move the branch while
it waits. Then it claims the main tree with its CLAUDE_WORKING flag, which
records $CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID, the time, and this host and process (an old-style
flag of this session's, one without a process, is kept), rebases again if the branch
moved while it waited, checks each commit as in step 4, once, and fast-forwards the
main tree with `git merge --ff-only`. A failing check releases the claim, merging
nothing. While another run holds the flag, this session's other runs (a subagent's)
included, it waits its turn in a first-come-first-served queue in the repo's common
git dir, printing a line when whom it waits on changes, and gives up after
WT_SHIP_WAIT seconds behind one holder, naming what held it up: the wait starts again
each time the holder changes, so a queue that moves never times out. --no-wait gives
up at once when the flag is held or anyone is queued. Under a lock in the queue folder,
it clears a flag, or an entry, whose process ran on this host and has ended; a wait of
FIFO_LOCK_SECONDS for that lock names the process holding it, and when that process is
stopped, how to continue it. A flag it can't judge, an old-style one or one from
another host, it only waits on.
Should the branch move during the checks anyway, from the owner or a tool that doesn't
read the flag, it rebases and checks again, up to 10 times, still holding the claim,
then merges. When the main tree's uncommitted changes, staged or not, or its untracked
files, touch the files the landing changes, it stops before any check, merging
nothing: ask the user. The flag it made, and its place in the queue, go once the merge
is done or has failed, or the
run is stopped. It must run in a linked worktree.

Environment:
  WT_SHIP_WAIT       seconds --local waits behind one holder of the main tree
                     (default 1800); the wait starts again when the holder changes
  WT_SHIP_POLL       seconds between its looks at the flag and the queue (default 5;
                     the tests shorten both)
  FIFO_LOCK_SECONDS  seconds of waiting for the queue folder's lock before naming its
                     holder (default 5; the tests shorten it)

Exit status: 0 shipped, landed or nothing to do; 1 a step failed (the message says
what to do); 2 a usage error; with --local, 3 another session held the main tree's
claim past the wait (tell the user what held it up and since when), 4 the main tree's
uncommitted changes touch the files the landing changes (ask the user); 5 the tries
ran out: another session's pushes winning each of 10, or with --local the branch
moving under the claim each time (run it again later).
"""

import argparse
import os
import re
import shlex
import signal
import subprocess
import sys
import time
from contextlib import contextmanager
from pathlib import Path
from typing import NamedTuple

sys.path.insert(0, str(Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent / "lib"))
import commitcheck
import fifo
from commitcheck import git, named, out
from fifo import Holder

# --local's outcomes the user decides on.
CLAIMED, SAME_FILES = 3, 4


class Stop(Exception):
    """A step failed; the message says what to do, and status is the exit status."""

    def __init__(self, message, status=1):
        super().__init__(message)
        self.status = status


GATE = Path(__file__).resolve().parent / "agent-commit-gate"

# This run's command line, so a stop can say to run the same thing again; main sets it.
RERUN = "wt-ship"


def worktree_hint(main_path):
    """How to run wt-ship on a worktree from the main tree."""
    return f"`wt-ship -C ../{Path(main_path).name}-wt-<topic>`"


class Mode(NamedTuple):
    """What a run does with the commits: for its messages, and how it rebases."""

    done: str  # "pushed" or "landed"
    # Rebase only the commits since HEAD forked from the branch, found through the
    # branch's reflog, so a commit another session rewrote meanwhile isn't replayed.
    fork_point: bool


PUSH = Mode("pushed", fork_point=False)
LAND = Mode("landed", fork_point=True)


def worktrees():
    """(path, branch or None) for each worktree, the main one first."""
    trees = []
    for block in out("worktree", "list", "--porcelain").split("\n\n"):
        lines = block.splitlines()
        path = next(ln[9:] for ln in lines if ln.startswith("worktree "))
        branch = next(
            (
                ln.removeprefix("branch refs/heads/")
                for ln in lines
                if ln.startswith("branch ")
            ),
            None,
        )
        trees.append((path, branch))
    return trees


def is_ancestor(older, newer):
    return git("merge-base", "--is-ancestor", older, newer, check=False).returncode == 0


def rebase(upstream, mode):
    env = os.environ | {"GIT_SEQUENCE_EDITOR": ":", "GIT_EDITOR": ":"}
    fork = ["--fork-point"] if mode.fork_point else []
    # Without git's own hint to `git add` and `git rebase --continue`, so the one
    # instruction is the message below, whose `conflicts --continue` checks first.
    run = subprocess.run(
        [
            "git",
            "-c",
            "advice.mergeConflict=false",
            "rebase",
            "-q",
            "-i",
            "--autosquash",
            *fork,
            upstream,
        ],
        env=env,
        capture_output=True,
        text=True,
        check=False,
    )
    if run.returncode != 0:
        raise Stop(
            f"{run.stdout}{run.stderr}".rstrip()
            + f"\nThe rebase onto {upstream} hit a conflict and is still in progress."
            "\nResolve each hunk (`conflicts FILE`, `conflicts -h`), then"
            " `conflicts --continue`, which refuses while any marker is left,"
            f" then run `{RERUN}` again."
        )


def markers(upstream):
    check = git("diff", "--check", upstream, "HEAD", check=False).stdout
    found = [
        line.removesuffix(": leftover conflict marker")
        for line in check.splitlines()
        if line.endswith(": leftover conflict marker")
    ]
    if found:
        raise Stop(
            "Leftover conflict markers; resolve them (`conflicts -h`) and amend, or for a"
            " file that holds them on purpose, set conflict-marker-size in"
            " .gitattributes:\n  " + "\n  ".join(found)
        )


CHECK = commitcheck.HOOK_NAME


def short(ref):
    """A branch's name as people say it: main, not refs/heads/main."""
    return ref.removeprefix("refs/heads/")


def upstream_note(upstream, hook):
    """For a failure at the first commit checked: a sentence when upstream's tip has
    no pass of the check either, so the failure may come from there."""
    if commitcheck.passed(upstream, hook):
        return ""
    return (
        f" {short(upstream)}'s tip has no pass of the check on record either, so the"
        " failure may come from there."
    )


def run_checks(upstream, mode):
    """Check every commit since upstream with the repo's agent check, if it has one:
    each with no pass on record, oldest first, each checked out here in turn, those
    that need heavy's queue in one job (at its front when landing, which runs under the
    main tree's claim); the first failure stops it."""
    hook = commitcheck.hook_path()
    if dropped := commitcheck.dropping_check(upstream, hook):
        raise Stop(
            f"{named(dropped)} has no {CHECK} in its own tree, an executable file or a"
            f" link that leads to one there, which {short(upstream)} has, so it would"
            f" land without the check. Put it back in that commit (`git commit"
            f" --fixup={dropped[:12]}`), then run `{RERUN}` again; nothing was"
            f" {mode.done}."
        )
    if not commitcheck.runnable(hook):
        print(f"No check: the repo has no {CHECK} hook.")
        return
    needed = commitcheck.unchecked(upstream, hook)
    if not needed:
        print(f"{CHECK}: every commit since {short(upstream)} passed it already.")
        return
    tip = out("rev-parse", "HEAD")
    count = f"{len(needed)} commit{'' if len(needed) == 1 else 's'}"
    print(
        f"Checking with {CHECK} the {count} with no pass on record, each checked out"
        " here in turn: until wt-ship ends, the work tree holds that commit, not the"
        " tip, so change or commit nothing in it.",
        flush=True,
    )
    failed = commitcheck.first_failing(hook, needed, GATE, front=mode is LAND)
    if failed is None:
        return
    note = upstream_note(upstream, hook) if failed == needed[0] else ""
    if failed == tip:
        raise Stop(
            f"The repo's {CHECK} fails at HEAD, {named(failed)}. Fix it, commit, then"
            f" run `{RERUN}` again; nothing was {mode.done}.{note}"
        )
    raise Stop(
        f"The repo's {CHECK} fails at {named(failed)}, a commit before HEAD. Fix it"
        f" with `git commit --fixup={failed[:12]}`, or move the code it needs into it,"
        f" then run `{RERUN}` again; nothing was {mode.done}, and the branch stays at"
        f" its tip.{note}"
    )


def refuse_a_marked_worktree():
    """Stop when another run is checking earlier commits here, or one was killed before
    it put the tip back."""
    found = commitcheck.marker()
    if found is None:
        return
    if not found.holder.gone():
        raise Stop(
            f"Another wt-ship ({found.holder}) is checking earlier commits in this"
            f" worktree: wait for it to end, then run `{RERUN}` again. If none is"
            f" running, delete {found.path}."
        )
    raise Stop(
        f"A wt-ship that was checking earlier commits here ({found.holder}) ended before"
        f" it put the tip, {found.tip[:12]}, back, so the work tree may hold one of them."
        f" Put the tip back with `{found.restore_command()}`, which discards changes to"
        f" tracked files, delete {found.path}, then run `{RERUN}` again."
    )


# A push that lost a race: refused as a non-fast-forward, or the branch moved mid-push.
REJECTED = (
    "[rejected]",
    "non-fast-forward",
    "fetch first",
    "incorrect old value",
    "cannot lock ref",
)


FIXUP_PREFIXES = ("fixup! ", "squash! ", "amend! ")
# A word of a commit message that may be a commit's ID: 7 to 64 hex digits, alone.
# git abbreviates to 7 by default; shorter words are mostly English (added, decade).
HEX_ID = "[0-9a-f]{7,64}"
COMMIT_ID = re.compile(rf"(?<![0-9A-Za-z_]){HEX_ID}(?![0-9A-Za-z_])")
# The line `git cherry-pick -x` adds, whose source is meant to stay off the branch.
CHERRY_PICKED = re.compile(rf"^\(cherry picked from commit ({HEX_ID})\)$", re.MULTILINE)
# How many times a push may lose a race to another session's, each followed by a
# rebase and the check, before wt-ship gives up; the same for a landing whose branch
# moves during the check.
TRIES = 10
# The exit status when the tries run out: nothing failed, so run it again later.
TRY_AGAIN = 5


def has_fixups(upstream):
    """Whether HEAD holds a commit to fold, since upstream."""
    subjects = out("log", "--format=%s", f"{upstream}..HEAD").splitlines()
    return any(subject.startswith(FIXUP_PREFIXES) for subject in subjects)


def not_replayed(upstream):
    """The commits a --fork-point rebase onto upstream leaves out: those upstream held
    when HEAD forked from it and holds no more, such as one another session rewrote
    or dropped. Empty when upstream's reflog has no fork point."""
    fork = git("merge-base", "--fork-point", upstream, "HEAD", check=False)
    if fork.returncode != 0:
        return []
    base = out("merge-base", upstream, "HEAD")
    point = fork.stdout.strip()
    if point == base:
        return []
    return out("log", "--reverse", "--format=%h %s", f"{base}..{point}").splitlines()


def prepare(upstream, mode):
    """Rebase onto upstream as rebase_onto does; check."""
    rebase_onto(upstream, mode)
    run_checks(upstream, mode)


class Citation(NamedTuple):
    """A commit since upstream whose message names commits upstream doesn't hold."""

    sha: str
    words: list[str]  # the IDs, as the message writes them
    # Whether one of them is a cherry-pick line's source that no ref holds at all,
    # which a ref would let pass.
    refless_pick: bool


def stale_citations(upstream):
    """A Citation for each commit since upstream, newest first, whose message names, by
    ID, commits of this repo that upstream doesn't hold: IDs a rebase will change, or
    has changed already. An ID on a cherry-pick line counts only when no lasting ref
    holds its commit (pick_source_holders)."""
    found = []
    landing = Landing(upstream)
    log = out("log", "-z", "--format=%H%n%B", f"{upstream}..HEAD")
    for entry in filter(None, log.split("\0")):
        sha, _, message = entry.partition("\n")
        words = dict.fromkeys(COMMIT_ID.findall(CHERRY_PICKED.sub("", message)))
        unheld = [word for word in words if unheld_commit(word, upstream)]
        picks = {}  # each cherry-pick line's ID that counts: whether no ref holds it
        for word in dict.fromkeys(CHERRY_PICKED.findall(message)):
            if word in words or not (commit := unheld_commit(word, upstream)):
                continue
            if landing.holds_a_copy(commit, sha):
                picks[word] = False
            elif not any(lasting := pick_source_holders(commit)):
                picks[word] = not lasting
        if unheld or picks:
            found.append(Citation(sha, unheld + list(picks), any(picks.values())))
    return found


class Landing:
    """The commits since upstream, which this landing's rebase copies."""

    def __init__(self, upstream):
        self.upstream = upstream
        self._patch_ids = None

    def holds_a_copy(self, commit, citing):
        """Whether `commit` is one of the landing's own, which its rebase rewrites:
        HEAD holds it, or it has the patch of one of them other than `citing`, the
        commit that names it, as the copy from before a rebase does."""
        if is_ancestor(commit, "HEAD"):
            return True
        if self._patch_ids is None:
            self._patch_ids = patch_ids(f"{self.upstream}..HEAD")
        others = {pid for sha, pid in self._patch_ids.items() if sha != citing}
        return patch_ids("-1", commit).get(commit) in others


def patch_ids(*revs):
    """{commit: patch ID} for the commits `git log REVS` lists, merges and commits with
    no change left out (they have no patch ID)."""
    log = out("log", "-p", "--no-color", "--no-ext-diff", "--no-merges", *revs)
    listed = subprocess.run(
        ["git", "patch-id", "--stable"],
        input=log,
        capture_output=True,
        text=True,
        check=True,
    ).stdout
    return {sha: pid for pid, sha in (line.split() for line in listed.splitlines())}


def unheld_commit(word, upstream):
    """The commit of this repo `word` resolves to when upstream doesn't hold it, else
    None. A partial clone would fetch an ID it lacks from its remote; it may not."""
    resolved = subprocess.run(
        ["git", "rev-parse", "--verify", "--quiet", f"{word}^{{commit}}"],
        env=os.environ | {"GIT_NO_LAZY_FETCH": "1"},
        capture_output=True,
        text=True,
        check=False,
    )
    commit = resolved.stdout.strip()
    if resolved.returncode != 0 or is_ancestor(commit, upstream):
        return None
    return commit


def pick_source_holders(commit):
    """For each branch, tag and remote-tracking ref that holds `commit`, whether it
    lasts: not a branch a linked worktree has checked out, nor that branch's twin on a
    remote (refs/remotes/REMOTE/BRANCH), which are a session's work that its landing
    rebases. A symbolic ref (origin/HEAD) is left out: the ref it names is listed."""
    worked = {branch for _, branch in worktrees()[1:] if branch}
    listed = out(
        "for-each-ref",
        "--contains",
        commit,
        "--format=%(refname) %(symref)",
        "refs/heads",
        "refs/tags",
        "refs/remotes",
    )
    lasting = []
    for line in listed.splitlines():
        ref, _, symref = line.partition(" ")
        if not symref:
            lasting.append(branch_of(ref) not in worked)
    return lasting


def branch_of(ref):
    """BRANCH for refs/heads/BRANCH or refs/remotes/REMOTE/BRANCH; None for a tag."""
    if ref.startswith("refs/heads/"):
        return ref.removeprefix("refs/heads/")
    if ref.startswith("refs/remotes/"):
        return ref.removeprefix("refs/remotes/").partition("/")[2]
    return None


def refuse_stale_citations(upstream, mode):
    """Stop when a commit message names, by ID, a commit upstream doesn't hold."""
    if found := stale_citations(upstream):
        raise Stop(
            f"These commit messages name, by ID, commits {short(upstream)} doesn't"
            " hold, and a rebase changes an unlanded commit's ID. Name each by its"
            " subject instead, with `build-state reword SHA` and the new message on"
            " stdin, newest commit first, as listed: rewording a commit changes the IDs"
            " of those after it.\n"
            + "\n".join(
                f"  {named(sha)}: names {', '.join(words)}; build-state reword"
                f" {sha[:12]}"
                for sha, words, _ in found
            )
            + (
                "\nAn ID on a `(cherry picked from commit ID)` line counts when no"
                " branch, tag or remote-tracking ref holds that commit: if it should"
                " last, give it one (`git tag NAME ID`, or a fetch into refs/remotes/)"
                " instead of rewording."
                if any(citation.refless_pick for citation in found)
                else ""
            )
            + f"\nThen run `{RERUN}` again; nothing was {mode.done}."
        )


def rebase_onto(upstream, mode):
    """Rebase onto upstream when HEAD lacks it, holds fixups to fold, or (--local)
    holds commits the branch dropped; refuse markers, and messages that name a commit
    upstream doesn't hold."""
    skipped = not_replayed(upstream) if mode.fork_point else []
    if skipped:
        print(
            f"Not replaying these, which {short(upstream)} held"
            " when this worktree forked from it and holds no more:\n  "
            + "\n  ".join(skipped)
            + "\n",
            flush=True,
        )
    if skipped or has_fixups(upstream) or not is_ancestor(upstream, "HEAD"):
        rebase(upstream, mode)
    markers(upstream)
    refuse_stale_citations(upstream, mode)


def rebase_and_check(remote, branch):
    """Fetch, rebase onto the remote branch, refuse markers, check."""
    git("fetch", "-q", remote, branch)
    upstream = f"{remote}/{branch}"
    prepare(upstream, PUSH)
    return upstream


def locate():
    """This worktree's path, the main tree's, the main tree's branch (None when
    detached), and whether this is the main tree."""
    here = os.path.realpath(out("rev-parse", "--show-toplevel"))
    main_path, main_branch = worktrees()[0]
    return here, main_path, main_branch, os.path.realpath(main_path) == here


def ship(args):
    refuse_a_marked_worktree()
    if git("status", "--porcelain", "--untracked-files=no").stdout:
        raise Stop("There are uncommitted changes: commit or stash them first.")
    if args.local:
        return land(args)
    here, main_path, main_branch, in_main = locate()
    if args.remove and in_main:
        raise Stop(
            "--remove works only on a linked worktree, not the main tree: name one"
            f" with -C, as {worktree_hint(main_path)}."
        )
    branch = args.branch or main_branch
    if not branch:
        raise Stop(
            "The main tree is on a detached HEAD: say which branch with --branch."
        )
    remote = args.remote or "origin"
    git("fetch", "-q", remote, branch)
    upstream = f"{remote}/{branch}"
    if is_ancestor("HEAD", upstream):
        print(f"HEAD is already in {upstream}: nothing to push.")
        return 0
    rebase_and_check(remote, branch)
    for attempt in range(1, TRIES + 1):
        old = out("rev-parse", upstream)
        pushed = git("push", "-q", remote, f"HEAD:{branch}", check=False)
        if pushed.returncode == 0:
            break
        if not any(sign in pushed.stderr for sign in REJECTED):
            raise Stop(f"The push failed:\n{pushed.stderr.strip()}")
        if attempt == TRIES:
            raise Stop(
                f"Another push won the race {TRIES} times running: run `{RERUN}` again"
                f" once {upstream} is quieter.\n{pushed.stderr.strip()}",
                status=TRY_AGAIN,
            )
        print(
            f"Another push won the race, so rebasing, checking and trying again"
            f" (try {attempt + 1} of {TRIES}):\n{pushed.stderr.strip()}",
            flush=True,
        )
        rebase_and_check(remote, branch)
    git("fetch", "-q", remote, branch)
    new = out("rev-parse", "HEAD")
    if not is_ancestor(new, upstream):
        raise Stop(f"{upstream} doesn't hold {new[:12]} after the push.")
    report(old, new, PUSH.done, branch, f"to {upstream}")
    if main_branch == branch:
        local = out("rev-parse", f"refs/heads/{branch}")
        if local != new and is_ancestor(local, upstream):
            print(
                f"\nThe main tree ({main_path}) is behind {upstream}: tell the user, who pulls there."
            )
    if args.remove:
        remove(main_path, here)
    return 0


def remove(main_path, here):
    os.chdir(main_path)
    git("worktree", "remove", here)
    print(f"Removed the worktree {here}.")


def changed_places(files):
    """The top-level files and directories among `files`, a directory with how many
    of them it holds, in the order git lists them, so a notice stays short: a skill
    is named by its own folder, since that says which skill changed."""
    # Each place in the order git first lists it; a file's count stays 0.
    counts: dict[str, int] = {}
    for path in files:
        parts = path.split("/")
        if len(parts) == 1:
            counts.setdefault(path, 0)
            continue
        depth = 2 if parts[0] == "skills" and len(parts) > 2 else 1
        folder = "/".join(parts[:depth]) + "/"
        counts[folder] = counts.get(folder, 0) + 1
    return ", ".join(
        f"{place} ({count})" if place.endswith("/") else place
        for place, count in counts.items()
    )


def report(old, new, verb, branch, where):
    subjects = out("log", "--format=%s", "--reverse", f"{old}..{new}").splitlines()
    files = out("diff", "--name-only", old, new).splitlines()
    print(f"{verb.capitalize()} {old[:7]}..{new[:7]} {where}.")
    print("\nNotice for other sessions:")
    print(
        f"FYI: {verb} {old[:7]}..{new[:7]} to {branch}: {'; '.join(subjects)}. Files: {changed_places(files)}."
    )


# The flag that claims a main tree, by the user's global instructions (GLOBAL_CLAUDE.md).
FLAG = "CLAUDE_WORKING"


def exclude_flag(main_path):
    """List FLAG in the main repo's info/exclude, so git never shows or commits it."""
    exclude = common_dir(main_path) / "info" / "exclude"
    exclude.parent.mkdir(exist_ok=True)
    text = exclude.read_text() if exclude.exists() else ""
    if FLAG not in text.splitlines():
        with exclude.open("a") as file:
            file.write(("" if text.endswith("\n") or not text else "\n") + f"{FLAG}\n")


# The queue of sessions waiting to land, in the repo's common git dir (lib/fifo.py).
QUEUE = "wt-ship-queue"


def wait_seconds():
    return float(os.environ.get("WT_SHIP_WAIT", "1800"))


def poll_seconds():
    return float(os.environ.get("WT_SHIP_POLL", "5"))


def common_dir(main_path):
    return Path(
        out("-C", main_path, "rev-parse", "--path-format=absolute", "--git-common-dir")
    )


@contextmanager
def claim(main_path, wait):
    """Hold the main tree's FLAG: kept when it's an old-style flag of this session's,
    made (and removed afterwards, while it still holds this run's text) when free.
    With wait, join the queue and take the flag on this session's turn, giving up
    after WT_SHIP_WAIT seconds behind one holder, the wait starting again whenever the
    holder changes; without, stop when it's held or anyone is queued. A
    flag or entry whose process has ended is cleared."""
    exclude_flag(main_path)
    path = Path(main_path) / FLAG
    me = Holder.mine()
    folder = common_dir(main_path) / QUEUE
    entry = None
    mine = None
    try:
        try:
            folder.mkdir(exist_ok=True)
            if wait:
                entry = fifo.join(folder, me)
        except OSError as error:
            raise Stop(
                f"Can't use the queue {folder}: {error.strerror}. Fix or remove it, then"
                f" run `{RERUN}` again."
            ) from None
        mine = take(path, folder, me, entry)
        yield
    finally:
        if entry is not None:
            entry.unlink(missing_ok=True)
        if mine is not None:
            fifo.release(path, mine)


def waiters_ahead(folder, entry):
    """The live waiters that joined before entry (all of them, without one), oldest
    first, as (entry path, holder); ended ones are removed."""
    return [(path, Holder.parse(text)) for path, text in fifo.ahead(folder, entry)]


def take(path, folder, me, entry):
    """Take the flag at path: the text it wrote, or None when it kept an old-style flag
    of this session's. Waits its turn in the queue when entry is set."""
    deadline = None
    behind = said = None
    while True:
        text = fifo.slot_text(path, folder)
        held = Holder.parse(text) if text is not None else None
        if held is not None and held.session == me.session and held.pid is None:
            return None
        if deadline is None or held != behind:
            # A new holder means the queue moved: wait WT_SHIP_WAIT anew.
            behind, deadline = held, time.monotonic() + wait_seconds()
        ahead = waiters_ahead(folder, entry)
        if held is None and not ahead:
            if not fifo.take(path, me.line(), folder):
                continue  # another session took it first: look again
            if entry is not None:
                entry.unlink(missing_ok=True)
            return me.line()
        if entry is None or time.monotonic() >= deadline:
            raise Stop(gave_up(path, held, ahead, entry is not None), CLAIMED)
        status = (
            f"{len(ahead)} ahead in the queue, the first {ahead[0][1]}"
            if ahead
            else "first in the queue"
        )
        if held is not None:
            status += f"; the main tree is held by {held}"
        if status != said:
            print(f"Waiting to land: {status}.", flush=True)
            said = status
        time.sleep(poll_seconds())


def gave_up(path, held, ahead, waited):
    """Why --local stopped without the main tree, naming what held it up."""
    seconds = wait_seconds()
    behind = " behind one holder" if held is not None else ""
    if not waited:
        took = ""
    elif seconds >= 60:
        took = f" after {seconds / 60:.0f} minutes{behind}"
    else:
        took = f" after {seconds:.0f} seconds{behind}"
    blockers = []
    if held is not None:
        blockers.append(f"the flag {path} ({held})")
    if ahead:
        first, holder = ahead[0]
        blockers.append(f"{len(ahead)} waiting ahead, the first {first} ({holder})")
    return (
        f"Stopped waiting for the main tree{took}: {' and '.join(blockers)}. Tell the user"
        " what held it up and since when, so they can clear one a crashed session left,"
        f" then run `{RERUN}` again."
    )


def refuse_overlap(main_path, ref):
    """Stop, with SAME_FILES, when the main tree's uncommitted changes touch a file this
    landing changes, either path of a rename on both sides: what's staged and what
    isn't, and untracked files git doesn't ignore, which the fast-forward would refuse
    to overwrite. NUL-separated, so a path git would quote matches as it is."""
    landing = out("diff", "--name-only", "--no-renames", "-z", ref, "HEAD")
    # .stdout, not out(): a leading space is the first entry's status letter.
    status = git(
        "-C",
        main_path,
        "status",
        "--porcelain",
        "-z",
        "--no-renames",
        "--untracked-files=all",
    ).stdout
    # Each entry is two status letters, a space, then the path.
    touched = {entry[3:] for entry in status.split("\0") if entry}
    if overlap := sorted((set(landing.split("\0")) - {""}) & touched):
        raise Stop(
            same_files("checked or merged", "\n  " + "\n  ".join(overlap)), SAME_FILES
        )


def same_files(spared, said):
    """The stop for the main tree's uncommitted changes in the way of a landing."""
    return (
        "The main tree's uncommitted changes touch files this landing changes, so"
        f" nothing was {spared}; ask the user:{said}"
    )


def merge(main_path, sha):
    """Fast-forward the main tree to sha."""
    run = git("-C", main_path, "merge", "-q", "--ff-only", sha, check=False)
    if run.returncode == 0:
        return
    said = f"{run.stdout}{run.stderr}".strip()
    if "would be overwritten" in said:
        raise Stop(same_files("merged", f"\n{said}"), SAME_FILES)
    raise Stop(f"git merge --ff-only failed in the main tree:\n{said}")


def land(args):
    """--local: rebase onto the main tree's branch, check, and fast-forward it."""
    if args.remote is not None:
        raise Stop(
            "--local lands on the main tree's branch: --remote doesn't go with it."
        )
    here, main_path, branch, in_main = locate()
    if in_main:
        raise Stop(
            "--local lands a linked worktree on the main tree's branch: name one with"
            f" -C, as {worktree_hint(main_path)}."
        )
    if not branch:
        raise Stop(
            "The main tree is on a detached HEAD: --local has no branch to land on."
        )
    if args.branch and args.branch != branch:
        raise Stop(
            f"--local lands on the branch the main tree has checked out ({branch}),"
            f" not {args.branch}."
        )
    ref = f"refs/heads/{branch}"  # a tag of the same name mustn't stand in for it
    if is_ancestor("HEAD", ref):
        print(f"HEAD is already in {branch}: nothing to land.")
        return 0
    # Before the wait, so a conflict shows without holding the main tree; no check
    # yet, since another landing may move the branch meanwhile.
    rebase_onto(ref, LAND)
    with claim(main_path, wait=not args.no_wait):
        rebase_onto(ref, LAND)
        # Before the checks, which everyone waiting for the claim waits through.
        refuse_overlap(main_path, ref)
        # Other sessions can't land now, so each commit is checked once.
        run_checks(ref, LAND)
        # The owner, or a tool that doesn't read the flag, may still move the
        # branch: rebase until HEAD holds it, checking each time, at most TRIES
        # checks in all.
        attempt = 1
        while not is_ancestor(ref, "HEAD"):
            arrived = out("log", "--oneline", f"HEAD..{ref}")
            if attempt == TRIES:
                again = f"`{RERUN}`"
                raise Stop(
                    f"{branch} moved during the check {TRIES} times running, so"
                    f" nothing was landed. What arrived last:\n{arrived}\n"
                    f"Find what keeps committing to {branch}, then run {again} again.",
                    status=TRY_AGAIN,
                )
            attempt += 1
            print(
                f"{branch} moved during the check, so rebasing and checking again"
                f" (try {attempt} of {TRIES}).",
                flush=True,
            )
            prepare(ref, LAND)
        old = out("merge-base", ref, "HEAD")
        new = out("rev-parse", "HEAD")
        merge(main_path, new)
        if not is_ancestor(new, ref):
            raise Stop(f"{branch} doesn't hold {new[:12]} after the merge.")
    report(old, new, LAND.done, branch, f"on {branch} in the main tree ({main_path})")
    if args.remove:
        remove(main_path, here)
    return 0


def main(argv=None):
    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(
        description=__doc__, formatter_class=argparse.RawDescriptionHelpFormatter
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "-C",
        dest="directory",
        metavar="DIR",
        help="run as if started in DIR, the worktree, so the call can start outside it",
    )
    parser.add_argument("--remote", help="the remote (default origin)")
    parser.add_argument(
        "--branch",
        help="the branch to push to (default: the main tree's; --local takes only that)",
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--remove",
        action="store_true",
        help="remove this worktree once the push or the landing is confirmed",
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--local",
        action="store_true",
        help="land on the main tree's branch instead of pushing (a repo that doesn't push)",
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--no-wait",
        action="store_true",
        help="with --local, stop at once when another session holds the main tree",
    )
    args = parser.parse_args(argv)
    global RERUN
    RERUN = shlex.join(["wt-ship", *(sys.argv[1:] if argv is None else argv)])
    if args.directory is not None:
        try:
            os.chdir(args.directory)
        except OSError as error:
            print(
                f"Can't run in {args.directory}: {error.strerror}. Give -C the"
                " worktree's folder.",
                file=sys.stderr,
            )
            return 2
    # A stopped run unwinds like a failed one, so a claim it made is released.
    for stop in (signal.SIGTERM, signal.SIGHUP, signal.SIGINT):
        signal.signal(stop, lambda signum, _frame: sys.exit(128 + signum))
    try:
        return ship(args)
    except Stop as error:
        print(error, file=sys.stderr)
        return error.status
    except commitcheck.GitError as error:
        print(error, file=sys.stderr)
        return 1


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
